{"hash":"129d509103803520","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":89.281,"said":"Om Namah Shivaya. Come, walk with me, down to the water’s edge. This calm sea is the Gulf of Mannar, and this shore, right in front of the temple’s east gate, is Agni Theertham. Every pilgrim begins here, with a bath in the sea. Now let me take you back to the Ramayana. Valmiki tells how Rama, his war in Lanka won, flew home with Sita in the flying chariot Pushpaka. As they passed over this coast, Rama pointed down and said: here, before the bridge was built, Mahadeva granted me his grace. And he named the place where the bridge began Setubandha, a holy ford that washes away even the gravest sins. Local tradition adds why this water bears the name of Agni, the god of fire: after Sita came unharmed through her ordeal by fire, Agni himself bathed here and prayed to Shiva. Now watch the pilgrims tie a little sand from this shore into a cloth. That sand is going on a long journey, all the way across India to Kashi, to be given to the Ganga. Keep it in mind; we will meet it again. Now turn around. The great gopuram is calling us up the street.\n\nDid you know? Some pilgrims take the sand for Kashi at Dhanushkodi, the island’s far tip, where the bridge to Lanka is said to have begun; the Kanchi Kamakoti Peetham did so in 2021.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1054},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1070,"end":1238}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,2],[0.314,0.312,3,8],[0.639,0.553,9,16],[2.127,0.421,18,22],[2.764,0.337,24,28],[3.113,0.204,29,33],[3.329,0.264,34,36],[3.834,0.325,38,42],[4.171,0.084,43,45],[4.267,0.12,46,49],[4.399,0.481,50,57],[4.892,0.361,58,62],[6.154,0.252,64,68],[6.418,0.312,69,73],[6.743,0.252,74,77],[7.007,0.132,78,80],[7.151,0.096,81,84],[7.26,0.325,85,89],[7.596,0.18,90,92],[7.788,0.517,93,99],[8.462,0.192,101,104],[8.666,0.132,105,109],[8.81,0.349,110,115],[9.447,0.337,117,122],[9.796,0.108,123,125],[9.916,0.3,126,131],[10.228,0.084,132,134],[10.325,0.1